<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cambyses II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/530-bce">530</a>–<a href="/years/522-bce">522 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cambyses II was the second king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, who ruled in the late sixth century BCE. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Pharaoh Psamtik III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelusium">Battle of Pelusium</a> in 525 BCE. Egypt was incorporated into the empire as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-seventh-dynasty-of-egypt">27th Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii.jpg" alt="Cambyses II" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_Cambyses_Apis_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cambyses was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> and Cassandane, and his younger brother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>. In April 538 BCE Cyrus appointed him governor of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and he received the royal sceptre in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> at the New Year festival. Cyrus dismissed him after nine months, for unknown reasons. Babylonian records give both men the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, King of the Lands, which suggests that Cyrus made him co-ruler some years before his campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>. Cambyses was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> before Cyrus fell in that campaign, and he had his father's body carried to Pasargadae for burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had been planned by Cyrus, who wished to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> with the Trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> lands. Cambyses seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, founded the Achaemenid navy with men and equipment from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the desert between Gaza and Egypt for water. In the spring of 525 BCE he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>, then besieged and captured Memphis. By summer all Egypt was under Persian suzerainty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of Cyrene and Barca acknowledged his authority, and he set a garrison at Elephantine against Kush. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> he was crowned in the temple of Neith and took the titles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>.</p><p>Greek writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> accuse him of brutality, of looting temples, of killing the sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apis">Apis bull</a>, and of madness. Pierre Briant holds that this bias casts doubt on Herodotus's account. An epitaph and sarcophagus show that Cambyses took part in the burial of an Apis, and the Demotic Chronicle says that he reduced the income of most Egyptian temples, which may explain hostile stories spread by priests. Herodotus also reports a disastrous expedition against Kush, but archaeology shows a Persian stronghold at Dorginarti.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 522 BCE Cambyses left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in haste to put down a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. On the way through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he received a wound in the thigh that became gangrenous, and he died about three weeks later, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>. Herodotus tells of an accident with his sword, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> of a self-inflicted wound, and some modern historians suspect assassination. He died childless and was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> soon overthrew. Cambyses was buried at Neyriz in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>. At his death the empire stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cambyses II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Cambyses II</b> defeated Pharaoh Psamtik III at the Battle of Pelusium in 525 BCE and incorporated Egypt, where the Persian kings count as its 27th Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His court attracted the poets Anacreon and Ibycus and the physician Democedes of Croton, and he made alliances first with Amasis of Egypt and later with <b>Cambyses II of Persia</b>, and around 522 BCE the Persian satrap Oroetes lured him to the mainland and had him killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Such service had its reward: for supporting <b>Cambyses II</b>'s conquest of Egypt the Persian king granted Sidon Dor, Joppa and the Plain of Sharon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He also says that when <b>Cambyses II</b> ordered an attack on Carthage, the Phoenicians refused to make war on their own children, and the plan was dropped.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The longest part is an account of the events that followed the death of <b>Cambyses II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Many non-Persians served the king too: the Egyptian priest and physician Udjahorresnet advised <b>Cambyses II</b> and Darius, and the Jewish courtier Nehemiah, by his own account, was sent to govern Judah.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-526" aria-label="Ecbatana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cambyses II"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4199 4549h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4199 4549h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-526">What was here in 526 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Cyrus the Great</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 600–530 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/atossa"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Atossa</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; c. 550–475 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah! truly, do what they may, it is impossible for men to turn aside the coming fate.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Persian nobles, dying, 522 BCE (Herodotus, Histories, book 3) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account; he had killed his brother on the strength of a dream &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do this, and then may your land bring you forth fruit abundantly, and your wives bear children, and your herds increase, and freedom be your portion for ever…&#8221;</p><footer>To the Persian nobles, dying, urging them to win back the throne from the Magi (Herodotus, Histories, book 3) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
